  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Quiet weather period with warming trend next\nweek.\n\nBroken to overcast cloud cover looks to linger for another day \nacross mountain locations with northwest flow continuing\nareawide. Areas east of the Blue Ridge look to remain mostly\nclear once again with downsloping ongoing through today. All\nsnow shower activity has primarily ended for upslope mountain\nlocations, with only isolated showers being observed in a few\nlocations west of the area tonight. All activity is expected to\nend by sunrise today. Little to no accumulation is expected\nthrough this period. \n\nSurface high pressure currently situated across Illinois and\neastern Iowa is expected to slide east into the region by Monday\nafternoon and evening. This will finally allow for completely\nclear skies for the entire region heading into the Monday\nafternoon and evening timeframe. As this happens, the gusty\nnorthwest winds that have been observed over the previous\nseveral days will also start to diminish throughout the day\ntoday and into Monday. With the aforementioned surface high\npressure settling over the region for through the end of the\nwork week, and likely into the weekend, the chance for\nprecipitation through the week is nearly zero percent areawide.\nAlong with dry conditions, 500mb heights will slowly increase\nacross the area each day starting Monday, which will lead to\nprogressively warmer days each day throughout the work week.\nHighs Sunday and Monday will generally be near average or\nslightly below average in the 40s/50s across the area, with\nwidespread 60s to near 70 overtaking the area by Tuesday across\nthe region. While daytime highs will warm up to these\ntemperatures, clear skies and calm winds will allow for\nefficient radiational cooling each night with some lows in the \n20s and gradually \"warming\" into the 30s.",
